<div class="learn-box">
**What you will learn**

Choose a populated glucose measurement, describe trajectories and observed
target attainment, and fit a small Gaussian model.
</div>

**You need:** [the installed packages and practice resources](book-00-start.html#before-you-start),
[concept discovery](book-04-vocabulary.html), and the preceding tutorial chapters.
Run these sections in order in a fresh `book-work` session. All observations are
public synthetic teaching data; the numerical results are not clinical evidence.

## Research question

Among Synthea people with blood-glucose measurements, how do first and last
values relate, and when is a declared teaching target first observed? We select
a lab-defined population, not a diagnosis-confirmed diabetes cohort.

```{r chapter-setup}
library(dsOMOPClient)
source("tools/book/display.R")
source("tools/book/connection.R")
source("tools/book/practice.R")
practice <- book_connect("numeric")
conns <- practice$conns
```

## Find the exact glucose concept

```{r glucose-discovery}
hits <- ds.omop.concept.search("Glucose", domain = "Measurement", vocabulary = "LOINC", conns = conns)$pooled
knitr::kable(hits[c("concept_name", "standard_concept", "concept_class_id")])
glucose_id <- hits$concept_id[trimws(hits$concept_name) == "Glucose [Mass/volume] in Blood"]
knitr::kable(ds.omop.concept.locate(glucose_id, scope = "pooled", conns = conns)$pooled)
knitr::kable(ds.omop.value.counts("measurement", "unit_concept_id", concept_id = glucose_id,
  scope = "pooled", conns = conns)$pooled)
glucose_set <- omop_concept_set(glucose_id)
```

This populated Blood concept differs from Serum or Plasma. Its recorded unit is
mg/dL. The categories below are public teaching thresholds, not treatment advice.

## Cohort, values and trajectories

```{r glucose-recipe}
glucose <- omop_event("measurement", glucose_set, unit = omop_unit(8840L))
first <- omop_variable(event = glucose, reducer = omop_reduce("first_value"))
last <- omop_variable(event = glucose, reducer = omop_reduce("last_value"))
cohort <- omop_population("measured", index = omop_index(glucose))
trajectory <- omop_recipe(populations = list(cohort), variables = list(
  first = first, last = last,
  control = omop_number_category(last, range = "thresholds", thresholds = c(70, 140)),
  change = omop_number_change(glucose),
  target_days = omop_number_change(glucose, "time_to_threshold", threshold = 140, op = "le")),
  outputs = omop_output("people", rows = "measured", format = "wide"))
print(recipe_execute(trajectory, out = c(people = "D_glucose"), conns = conns))
knitr::kable(book_summary(dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_glucose$change", datasources = conns)))
```

All available history contributes to first and last. A difference in person-level
values is not a slope or an intervention effect. Selecting people with a test can
also select on disease severity and healthcare use.

## Categories and time to target

The prescribed three-level rule produces a small category in this tiny sample.
This deliberately shows a refusal: the table is withheld, not a zero-frequency
result. Keep the planned thresholds and report that limitation.

<!-- expected-refusal:start -->
```{r glucose-control}
control_message <- tryCatch(ds.omop.table("D_glucose", "control", conns = conns),
  error = function(e) conditionMessage(e))
print(control_message)
```
<!-- expected-refusal:end -->

An observed target time is missing for someone never observed below the threshold.
Day zero means already meeting it at the first reading. Measurement schedules
can move observed attainment later than the underlying biological change.

```{r glucose-target}
knitr::kable(book_summary(dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_glucose$target_days", datasources = conns)))
```

## Model the last value

Fit last glucose against first glucose. This small synthetic Gaussian model
teaches a workflow; it is not a validated diabetes risk model. The local-start
warnings are the documented dsBase setup behaviour, not hidden output.

<!-- expected-glm-warning:start -->
```{r glucose-model}
glucose_fit <- dsBaseClient::ds.glm("last ~ first", data = "D_glucose",
  family = "gaussian", datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(glucose_fit$coefficients)
```
<!-- expected-glm-warning:end -->

## Exercises

**1.** Summarise first glucose in its declared unit.

<details><summary>Solution 1</summary>

```{r glucose-first}
knitr::kable(book_summary(dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_glucose$first", datasources = conns)))
```

The unit is mg/dL. A unit declaration must match the recorded source.

</details>

**2.** Explain why target-time missingness should not be replaced with zero.

<details><summary>Solution 2</summary>

```{r glucose-target-definition}
trajectory$variables$target_days$derived[c("method", "threshold", "op", "reference")]
```

Zero represents immediate observed attainment. Missing instead means no observed
attainment in the eligible records; a censored analysis needs follow-up times too.

</details>

**3.** Inspect the fitted sample size and convergence information.

<details><summary>Solution 3</summary>

```{r glucose-diagnostics}
glucose_fit[c("Nvalid", "Nmissing", "iter", "errorMessage")]
```

A successful recipe does not guarantee enough complete observations for a model.
Keep diagnostics beside the coefficients in a study record.

</details>

## Recap

Find a populated measurement, state the selected population, define units and
thresholds, preserve temporal meaning, then interpret a model within those choices.
Rare categories and small complete-case samples can limit what is releasable.

**Next:** [22. Case study: drug utilisation](book-22-case-drug.html).
